<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">8198991</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">1969441</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Selective toxicity of TGF-alpha-PE40 to EGFR-positive cell lines: selective protection of low EGFR-expressing cell lines by EGF.</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Kirk</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Carmichael</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Stratford</surname><given-names>I. J.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Harris</surname><given-names>A. L.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff>ICRF Laboratories, Institute of Molecular Medicine, Headington, UK.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>6</month><year>1994</year></pub-date><volume>69</volume><issue>6</issue><fpage>988</fpage><lpage>994</lpage><abstract><p>The sensitivity of human breast and lung cancer cell lines to TGF-alpha-PE40, a novel chimeric recombinant cytotoxin composed of two independent domains, (i) TGF-alpha and (ii) a 40 kDa segment of the Pseudomonas exotoxin protein, PE-40, was investigated. Toxicity varied widely, correlated with epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) levels (P = 0.01) and was greatly reduced by EGF, indicating that binding of TGF-alpha-PE40 to EGFR is important in mediating toxicity. Cell lines expressing low EGFR levels were most highly protected by EGF, indicating that normal (low EGFR-expressing) tissue may be selectively protected by EGF in vivo. P-glycoprotein did not confer resistance to TGF-alpha-PE40, and toxicity was unaffected by multidrug resistance-modulating agents (cyclosporin A, tamoxifen, verapamil), indicating a role for TGF-alpha-PE40 in the clinical management of drug-resistant tumours.</p></abstract></article-meta></front><body><supplementary-material content-type="scanned-pages"><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00196-0012.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="988" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00196-0013.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="989" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00196-0014.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="990" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00196-0015.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="991" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00196-0016.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="992" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00196-0017.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="993" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00196-0018.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="994" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/></supplementary-material></body></article>